\section{Complete client histories}{

Two further client-level filters were applied, so that for every client in
the data the number of loans observed equals the highest
\code{loan_seq_number} among their issued applications. Clients failing
that check were removed -- 1,507 clients, 7,909 rows. The full observation
window is retained; no clients were dropped on first-appearance date.

The reason is \strong{left truncation}. The underlying extract is a window,
not the lender's whole history, so a client already borrowing when the
window opened carries a \code{loan_seq_number} counting loans that are not
in the data. Before filtering, 53\% of mismatching clients first appeared in
the window's opening month, with a median of four unobservable prior loans.

The practical consequence: \code{loan_seq_number}, \code{loans_repaid_*},
\code{lifetime_*} and \code{prev_loan_*} can all be trusted as complete for
every client here. A client with \code{loan_seq_number == 1} really is a
first-time borrower. That is unusual in real portfolio data and is what
makes this dataset usable for repeat-borrower and lifetime-value work.

The cost is that the data is no longer a random sample of the portfolio:
long-tenured clients are systematically absent, so the population skews
toward newer borrowers.
}

\section{Known limitations}{

\itemize{
  \item \strong{No requested or offered principal.} The source extract does
    not carry the amount the client asked for, nor the amount offered. For
    rejected and cancelled applications there is therefore no loan-size
    variable at all; \code{principal_disbursed} exists only where a loan was
    issued.
  \item \code{loan_id} is redundant here: the product is single-pay, so it
    is 1:1 with issued applications and carries no information beyond
    \code{application_id} and \code{application_status}. It is retained only
    so loan-level aggregation has a key to dispatch on.
  \item \strong{Currency amounts are unscaled} and in the original currency.
  \item \strong{Bureau and device columns are 75-95\% missing.} That reflects
    real data-acquisition policy (the lender did not buy every feed for every
    application), so treat missingness as informative rather than as noise.
  \item \code{number_of_children} had its top category \code{"5AndMore"}
    replaced with a random draw, so that column carries a small amount of
    synthetic variation at the top end.
  \item \strong{Stored precision was deliberately reduced} to keep the file
    a reasonable size: timestamps are rounded to the minute (the source
    recorded microseconds) and \code{client_age} to whole years (the source
    carried 13 decimal places). Neither rounding affects any analysis the
    package supports, but do not treat the timestamps as exact.
